Stick with .osm.pbf.

Using PBF for intermediate steps offers several distinct advantages:

    File Size and Speed: PBF files are compressed binary streams. A regional XML extract can balloon to gigabytes very quickly, whereas the PBF equivalent is a fraction of the size and vastly faster to read and parse.

    Lossless Storage: PBF retains 100% of the OSM data attributes (nodes, ways, relations, and all tags) with no precision loss.

    Tool Compatibility: Almost all modern OSM tools (including osmium itself, osmium-tool, and language bindings in Python, C++, etc.) read PBF natively and much faster than XML.

Streamlining Your Next Step

If your ultimate goal is to get filtered tags into a JSON format, you can often bypass writing a custom parsing script entirely by using osmium export.

Instead of doing a separate tag-extraction step followed by manual JSON conversion, osmium export can read your .osm.pbf file, filter specific tags via a configuration file, and output GeoJSON directly in a single command:
Bash

osmium export buffalo_clip.osm.pbf -o buffalo_features.geojson

